wg-manage is a command line tool to centrally organize your wireguard configuration.
[add | a] -name {peer-1} [-ip {} | auto] [-server -endpoint {}] -persistent {false} -add-routing {false} -force {false} -config {config.yaml}
	Add a new record to the yaml file; without -ip the address is auto-allocated from settings.ipPool
	Example: wg-manage a -name MyHomeComputer (auto IP) or wg-manage a -name MyHomeComputer -ip 10.0.2.10
[bootstrap | b] -endpoint {some.server.somewhere:51820} -persistent {false} -port {0=auto} -preshared {none|single|per-peer} -output {config.yaml}
	Creates a simple, ready to run wireguard network configuration with one server and two clients, 'kill-switch' (all client traffic goes through wireguard, including external) and NAT.
	Assumes 10.0.2.0/24 CIDR, wg0 and eth0 on server.
	Example: wg-manage b -endpoint myhome.someddnsprovider.com
[generate | g] -output {./output} -png {true} -config {config.yaml}
	Generates config files from yaml file
	Example: wg-manage g -config my-wireguard.yaml
[init | i] -clients {1} -servers {1} -output {config.yaml}
	Initialize basic configuration with specified number of clients and servers
	Requires further editing of the resulting yaml file with endpoint information.
	Example: wg-manage i -clients 10 -servers 2
[remove | r] [-name {} | -key {}] -config {config.yaml}
	Removes a record from the config file by name or public key
	Example: wg-manage r -name MyHomeComputer
[format | f] -input {config.yaml}
	Formats config file to stdout
[recreate | rc] -config {config.yaml}
	(*DANGEROUS*) Recreates all private and public keys (*DANGEROUS*)
	(outputs to stdout for safety reasons)
[pool | p] -config {config.yaml}
	Shows the IP pool, subnets, used addresses (with owning peer) and free addresses
	Example: wg-manage p
[psk] [-mode {status|single|per-peer|remove}] -config {config.yaml}
	Manage preshared keys: status (default), single global key, unique per-peer keys, or remove all
	Example: wg-manage psk -mode per-peer
[tui | t] -config {config.yaml}
	Interactive terminal UI: manage peers, routing, overrides and settings without editing yaml
	Example: wg-manage tui
[viz | v] -config {config.yaml} -format {ascii|dot|mermaid}
	Visualizes the configured wireguard network (servers, clients, overrides, free addresses)
	Example: wg-manage v -format dot | dot -Tsvg > network.svg
